{"step_index":9227,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T04:10:15Z","thinking":"The initial statement regarding direct syscalls allowing arbitrary file deletion seems overstated. The accurate understanding centers on a process potentially bypassing userland wrappers or hooks to remove a file if the process has the necessary Linux permissions or access rights for that specific file.\n\n\nDirect system calls bypass application-level interception, but kernel permission checks still apply; they are not a complete security bypass. Azure locks, while preventing in-VM root processes without credentials from directly modifying resources, don't guarantee full account security, as external administrator access represents a separate trust boundary. A combination of `sudo NOPASSWD: ALL` and `NoNewPrivs: 0` can lead to a complete trust boundary compromise. VM-internal delete proof is failing while cloud-plane deletion protection is conditionally protected.\n\n\nThe \"100% Delete-Proof\" claim is now invalidated, reclassifying it to \"FAILED\" for VM-internal protection, and \"CONDITIONALLY PROTECTED\" for cloud-plane deletion protection. Vulnerabilities stem from bypassable userland protections and unrestricted sudo privileges, coupled with the absence of Landlock LSM. Even immutable files can be renamed within writable parent directories. The Azure Cloud-Plane remains the only secure boundary from the VM context due to credential restrictions.\n\n\nA confirmed vulnerability exists, allowing file deletion via direct system calls, bypassing userland protections. Unrestricted sudo privileges and the lack of Landlock LSM significantly increase the attack surface. Even immutable files are susceptible to renaming if their parent directory is writable.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"replace_file_content","args":{"AllowMultiple":"false","Description":"\"Update executive summary with precise calibrated status and root trust boundary flaw\"","